                <pb facs="0109" n="103" sameAs="frus:frus1894app1/pg103"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 103.—Translation from
                        Japanese.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Reading of a telegram received from Lieut. M. J.
                        O’Brien, military attaché of the United States legation, Tokyo.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Port Arthur</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-02-19T10:50:00+08:05"><hi rendition="#italic">February 19,
                                1895</hi>—10.50 a.m.</date><lb/><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Fusan</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic">February 20, 1895</hi>—12.05
                        p.m.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">American Minister</hi>, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Tokyo:</hi></p>
                <p>The Chinese army has surrendered the forts on Liu-kung tao (island), together
                    with the remainder of the fleet. The Japanese torpedo boats have sunk the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ping-Yuen</hi> and several armored cruisers besides. Two
                    of the Japanese torpedo boats also received injuries. Another Chinese cruiser
                    was sunk by shells. On the night of the 6th 13 Chinese torpedo boats attempted
                    to escape, but before reaching Chefoo were either sunk or captured. According to
                    the Chinese reports Admiral Ting and several officers have committed suicide.
                    The number of surrendered Chinese <pb facs="0110" n="104" xml:id="pg104"/>
                    soldiers is 2,400. The intention is to keep the officers prisoners, and to let
                    the soldiers go outside the Japanese lines, taking away their arms, and
                    furnishing them with two days’ rations. These matters should be reported to the
                    American War Department by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed>Lieutenant <hi rendition="#small-caps">O’Brien</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Attaché of American Legation.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
